Z||000015||WKDFIRO||04-07-2001||05:34 AM||||The pictures were most likely going for model year '90 Fiero. The prototype in the photos was not quite ready for 89 production yet. It was only a presentation car for the next generation of Fieros. If you look at it closely (plus Fiero Phil Huff has some pictures of this 90 Fiero that are are not on the grass but on the nearby viewing yard)it was not only longer but also wider than the production Fieros at the time. The front and rear fenders actually went past the side view mirrors radius. The mirrors would actually look at the rear flanks. (gotta love that!)<P>Also the chassis was the same more or less based on a video presentation of the 90 Fiero used to show production hurtles. An extension piece needed to be added near the quarter panels to support the fenders of the GT. The trunk had its own lid (like the first MR-2s) and the engine bay hinged at the top of the B-pillar. The "fast back" portions went up with it! The interior was still being decided but as I understand one of the interior proposals was installed in this prototype. Ive never seen pictures of the interior though.<P>Phil quietly popped this video in during one of our Christmas parties and you would have thought he had put in a porno the way everyone stopped everything to watch!<BR>The video presentation was being given by Hulki Aldikacti (The Father of the Fiero) and no one spoke a word!<P>It would seem that the Fiero was going to get bigger and its very exciting to speculate, looking at how far they pushed the trunk back, if they were getting ready to "push the button" on Chevy and widen the engine bay...a bigger Fiero would mean a heavier Fiero and a heavier Fiero would mean a bigger motored Fiero...<P>Hmmmmm....the 2.8s were fazed out in the early 90s for the 3.4 until the late 90s when the 3800 came in (The 3.4 DOHC being dabbled with and now gone). GM being the cheap bastards they are could have given the Fiero the 3.4 that the f-bodies had and then later taken the already proven front wheel drive 3800 (dare we dream the 3800 SC?) and plunk it down in the back. The argument could have been that the Vette keeps the V8 and the Fiero GT(P?) goes only as "powerful" as the 3800 Supercharged(currently only 240 hp I believe The Vette is considerably higher). The Firebird could be fazed out earlier than it is now because the new Fiero would be taking its place and probably beat out its sales. Camaros could keep its market section and Pontiac gets to play in the performance segment! <P>Lets all just take a moment and just smile at what a 98 Fiero GTP would have been like...<P>Sigh............<P>(someday I will learn how to write a short reply...)<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 04-07-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000016||Formula88||07-30-2001||11:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WKDFIRO:<BR><B>Lets all just take a moment and just smile at what a 98 Fiero GTP would have been like...<P>Sigh............(someday I will learn how to write a short <P>reply...)<P>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 04-07-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah.  The 98 Fiero GTP could have been comparable to the Acura NSX.  That's where it could have gone, anyway.  {{sigh}}||||reg||1
